## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses hands-on planning expertise across REST and WebSocket streaming, tenant isolation, contracts, and resilience—areas often under-tested in real-time systems but critical for production reliability.

## Key Concepts

- **Contract Testing**: Validate agreements between gateway and downstream services using PACT or similar frameworks to ensure API compatibility.
- **WebSocket Streaming**: Test connectivity, message ordering, heartbeats, and backpressure handling under load.
- **Per-Tenant Isolation**: Verify data separation through keys, partitions, and role-based access controls to prevent cross-tenant leakage.
- **Fault Injection**: Test circuit breakers, retry logic, and fallback mechanisms when downstream services throttle or fail.
- **Observability**: Implement OpenTelemetry traces, structured logs, and dashboards for visibility into system behavior.
- **End-to-End SLAs**: Validate latency, throughput, and data integrity requirements per tenant.

## Code Example

```java
@Test
void shouldIsolateTenantsInWebSocketStream() {
    String tenantA = "tenant-a-key";
    String tenantB = "tenant-b-key";

    WebSocketClient clientA = new WebSocketClient(wsUrl, tenantA);
    WebSocketClient clientB = new WebSocketClient(wsUrl, tenantB);

    clientA.connect();
    clientB.connect();

    clientA.send("telemetry:update", payloadA);
    clientB.send("telemetry:update", payloadB);

    assertThat(clientA.receive()).doesNotContain(payloadB);
    assertThat(clientB.receive()).doesNotContain(payloadA);
}
```

## Test Cases

| Category | Test Case | Acceptance Criteria |
|----------|-----------|---------------------|
| REST | Concurrent tenant requests | Each tenant receives only their own data |
| WebSocket | Backpressure under high message rate | Messages queue without dropping or timing out |
| Contract | Gateway matches downstream schema | PACT verification passes |
| Resilience | Downstream timeout | Gateway returns 503 within SLA |
| Throttling | Rate limit exceeded | 429 with proper retry-after header |

## Why This Is Asked

Tests for idempotent POSTs prevent duplicate orders when clients retry. It shows understanding of race conditions, state management, and how to validate that retries are safe across failure modes.

## Key Concepts

- Idempotency keys
- Duplicate detection and database invariants
- Retry semantics and safe side effects
- Observability and tooling

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example idempotent POST test (pseudo)
async function testIdempotentPost() {
  const key = 'abcd-1234';
  const payload = { customerId: 'c1', items: [{sku:'A', qty:1}] };
  const r1 = await fetch('/orders', { method:'POST', headers:{'Idempotency-Key': key}, body: JSON.stringify(payload) });
  const r2 = await fetch('/orders', { method:'POST', headers:{'Idempotency-Key': key}, body: JSON.stringify(payload) });
  // asserts: r1.status==201, r2.status==200 and r1.json().orderId===r2.json().orderId
}
```

